(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physical-diagnosis-2-exam-1-ocular-exam-quiz"]={"title":"Advanced Ocular History & Examination Quiz 1 — PD2 Exam 1","category":"Physical Diagnosis","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Eye pain WITH HEADACHE suggests what?","choices":["Conjunctivitis","Acute narrow angle glaucoma","Temporal arteritis with jaw claudication","Optic neuritis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the pairing for headache plus eye pain. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"On the red-eye chart, how is the PAIN of acute iritis described?","choices":["Severe, aching, deep","Absent","Moderate, aching, deep","Mild discomfort rather than pain"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the chart's iritis column. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 48 (image only)"},{"q":"Which nerve mediates the pupillary constriction of the near reaction, and which two things accompany it?","choices":["The oculomotor nerve; accompanied by convergence and accommodation","The optic nerve; accompanied by convergence and dilation","The trochlear nerve; accompanied by depression and intorsion","The abducens nerve; accompanied by abduction"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the material notes convergence and accommodation are coincident but not part of the constriction. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"Which complaints fall in the URGENT group, seen by ophthalmology within a day or less?","choices":["Pinguecula and pterygium","Acute glaucoma","Sudden vision loss, chemical burns and suspected globe rupture","Dry eye and blepharitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the urgent list. Orbital cellulitis, corneal ulcer or abrasion, retinal detachment, macular oedema or haemorrhage, and hyphaema. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 124"},{"q":"The chart warns that the eye in a ciliary-injection condition may be diffusely red instead. Which other clues does it give to catch those conditions?","choices":["Pain, decreased vision, unequal pupils","Itch, watery discharge and lid crusting","Bilateral involvement together with a tender preauricular node","Fever and periorbital swelling"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the chart's four backup clues when the injection pattern is unhelpful. A less than perfectly clear cornea. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 48 (image only)"},{"q":"How is the static finger wiggle test performed?","choices":["Covering and uncovering each eye in turn","At arm's length","Moving a red-topped pin inward from beyond the boundary of each quadrant","Shining a light alternately into each eye"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the technique in full. Hands held two feet apart lateral to the ears, wiggling the fingers and bringing them slowly into the centre of view until seen, testing each quadrant or each hour of the clock. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Where does a chalazion usually point?","choices":["At the lateral canthus","Inside the lid rather than on the lid margin","On the lid margin, right at the base of the lashes","At the medial canthus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the material notes this as a distinguishing feature. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"At what setting can an ophthalmoscope be used to look for a corneal ulcer, and what is more sensitive?","choices":["Set at +40 dioptres, giving roughly a ten times magnifier","Set at zero dioptres — and this is more sensitive than fluorescein","Set at minus 20 dioptres — with fluorescein being less sensitive","No ophthalmoscope setting is useful here"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — it is both the setting and the better test. But staining with fluorescein is more sensitive for early ulcers. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 123"},{"q":"Eye pain WITH BLINKING suggests what?","choices":["Acute narrow angle glaucoma","Optic neuritis","Temporal arteritis","Corneal abrasion or a foreign body"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the lid moving over the defect is what hurts. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"On the red-eye chart, what does it give as the significance of the glaucoma column?","choices":["Bacterial, viral and other infections, or allergy and irritation","Abrasions and other injuries; viral and bacterial infections","Often none; may result from trauma, bleeding disorders, or a sudden increase in venous pressure","An acute increase in intraocular pressure — an emergency"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the chart labels it an emergency outright. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 48 (image only)"},{"q":"Acute, unilateral and PAINLESS visual loss suggests which group of causes?","choices":["Simple glaucoma or cataract","Retinal vascular occlusion, retinal detachment, vitreous haemorrhage, macular degeneration","Corneal abrasion or ulcer, uveitis, traumatic hyphaema, acute narrow angle glaucoma","Thermal, radiation or chemical exposure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the painless group. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Nodular episcleritis is associated with which two systemic diseases?","choices":["Graves disease, myasthenia gravis and thyroid orbitopathy","Sjögren syndrome and sarcoidosis","Rheumatoid arthritis and lupus erythematosus","Diabetes and hypertension"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the two associations. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"On the red-eye chart, which column has NO ocular discharge AND an unaffected pupil AND a clear cornea AND absent pain?","choices":["Subconjunctival haemorrhage","Uncomplicated bacterial conjunctivitis","Acute iritis","Glaucoma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — every column reads negative, which is what makes it benign. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 48 (image only)"},{"q":"What must be documented when the pupils are dilated?","choices":["The time of dilation and the agents used","The visual acuity before dilation only","The patient's consent form number","Nothing specific"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — it is both. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"Which complaints fall in the EMERGENT group, referred to ophthalmology immediately?","choices":["Sudden vision loss, retinal artery occlusion, chemical burns, rupture, acute angle-closure glaucoma, and vitreous haemorrhage","Acute glaucoma, orbital cellulitis, corneal ulcer or abrasion, retinal detachment, macular oedema, and hyphaema","Blepharitis, chalazion and dry eye","Conjunctivitis and subconjunctival haemorrhage"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the emergent list, which Beck asked the class to know. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 124"},{"q":"Bilateral visual loss usually implies which kind of problem?","choices":["A primary neurologic cause rather than a primary ophthalmologic one","A primary ophthalmologic cause rather than a neurologic one","An infective cause","A traumatic cause"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and this point is stated explicitly. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 10 (speaker notes)"},{"q":"When must pupillary reactions be checked, relative to dilation?","choices":["After dilating","It makes no difference","Only after the fundoscopic examination","Before dilating"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the material notes this explicitly in the suggested order. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Once local eye disease is excluded, which three causes of a dilated pupil remain?","choices":["Optic neuritis, papilloedema and optic atrophy","Compression or other lesion of the third cranial nerve","Glaucoma, cataract and macular degeneration","Horner syndrome, myasthenia gravis and Graves disease"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the three, and Beck called them important to know. Parasympathetic denervation from a ciliary ganglion lesion producing an Adie's pupil; and pharmacologic block of the pupillary sphincter. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"Which two structures are commonly left unexamined?","choices":["The eyelids and eyebrows","The lacrimal sac and puncta","The extraocular muscles","The cornea and the lens"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — 'failure to examine the cornea and lens' is on the list. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"How does the SIZE of the striking object change the injury?","choices":["Object size makes no difference","Only velocity matters, not size","Larger objects transfer most of the energy to the orbital rim","Larger objects strike the globe directly; smaller objects hit the orbital rim"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the mechanical distinction. Smaller objects may strike the globe directly. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 111"},{"q":"Besides faulty alignment, what causes diplopia?","choices":["Dry eye","Cataract","A neurological problem","Refractive error alone, without a neurological component"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the neurological grouping. Lesions in the brainstem or cerebellum, or weakness or paralysis of one or more extraocular muscles. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"An acute, significantly dilated pupil is a medical emergency particularly when accompanied by what, and from which two causes?","choices":["Headache or other neurologic signs","Itching or discharge — from an allergic conjunctivitis","Fever — from orbital cellulitis","Blurred vision alone — from cataract"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and it is flagged in red as a point to notice. From uncal herniation or a posterior communicating artery aneurysm. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"Where do xanthelasma plaques sit, and what should they prompt?","choices":["At the lid margin — consider bacterial infection","On the bulbar conjunctiva — consider ultraviolet exposure","Along the NASAL portions of one or both eyelids — consider lipid disorders","Along the temporal portions of the lids — consider thyroid disease"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — it is both the position and the inference. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Purulent discharge suggests what?","choices":["Viral conjunctivitis","Dry eye","Bacterial infection","Allergic conjunctivitis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the pairing for purulent discharge. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"How does fluorescein reveal a corneal defect?","choices":["The dye stains intact epithelium and spares defects","The dye is injected intravenously and photographed with a blue flash","The dye changes the colour of the aqueous humour","Orange dye is instilled and a blue light used"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — uptake marks where the epithelium is missing. The dye is taken up by areas of cornea devoid of epithelium. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 121"},{"q":"Which immunisation status matters in eye trauma?","choices":["Hepatitis B","Tetanus","Influenza","Pneumococcal"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — it is tetanus status specifically for eye trauma. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"How do you assess whether the eyes are truly protruding?","choices":["Palpate the globe to judge its forward position","Stand behind the seated patient and inspect from above","Sit facing the patient and compare the two eyes directly","Measure the distance from the lateral orbital angle with a ruler"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the technique Beck demonstrated and kept in scope. Drawing the lid slightly upward to compare the corneas against the lower lids. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What happens when a mydriatic is instilled in an Argyll Robertson pupil?","choices":["Full and rapid dilation","No change at all","Paradoxical constriction","Incomplete dilation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the material notes mydriatics dilate it only incompletely. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"When is globe rupture more likely in a patient with a subconjunctival haemorrhage?","choices":["When the haemorrhage is small, flat and confined to one sector","When the patient is anticoagulated","When both eyes are affected","In trauma, and when the haemorrhage encircles the entire cornea"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — it is both the mechanism and the 360-degree pattern. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What is the vital sign of the eye?","choices":["Pupil reactivity","Visual fields","Visual acuity","Intraocular pressure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the material labels acuity testing the vital sign of the eye. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 21"}]};
